      "entityUrn": "urn:li:fsd_update:(urn:li:sponsoredContentV2:(urn:li:activity:7260764318273622016,urn:li:sponsoredCreative:1234698834),MAIN_FEED,EMPTY,DEFAULT,true)",
      "commentary": "Here is the first clip of an upcoming series called \"things you should know about SOC 2\". This is our first run, so be kind \ud83d\ude05 \n\nIn this clip I explain that SOC 2 is a descriptive framework. Descriptive frameworks are made to give the freedom to companies to decide for themselves what they want to do in terms of security, and then have an auditor verify that they do what they claim they do. SOC 2, in a sense, is a CPA verified description of your company and its security measures.\n\nThis is very different from prescriptive frameworks, like ISO 27001, that tell you exactly what you should be doing.\n\nDescriptive frameworks come with obvious pros and cons.\n\nPros:\n- You get the flexibility to make a security program as lightweight or extensive as you see fit and deem necessary\n- You can prove aspects of security that fall outside the scope of prescriptive frameworks.\n\nCons:\n- It is easy to cheat. Companies can go with the most lightweight program possible, without investing in real security, and get a fully valid SOC 2 report. Many platforms serve default programs that are like that.\n- It is harder to parse. When you're given a SOC 2 report, you won't know what a company does in terms of security until you go through it with a fine-toothed comb.\n\nIt is important to keep these pros and cons in mind, both when pursuing compliance as well as when you're evaluating vendor security. No two SOC 2 reports are alike, so you should always be on the lookout for cookie-cutter programs that are a rubber stamp instead of validation of a solid security program.",
      "actorName": "Bryan Onel",
